i='Create a beat sheet for a story chapter.\n\nSTYLE: SCREENWRITER-STYLE DRAMATIC BEATS\nWrite like a scene breakdown for production: concrete objectives, visible blocking, interruptions, failed attempts, and hooks \u2014 not novelistic interiority or essay voice.\n\nINPUTS (only these \u2014 no editorial critique of prose is supplied):\n- Story bible\n- Codex summary (compressed references)\n- Chapter metadata (title, goal, logline)\n- Chapter sketch (rough summary / outline-level)\n- Structural diagnostics JSON when present (machine-facing); otherwise infer structure without it\n\nDo NOT invent "critique notes", writing-quality feedback, or thematic analysis as a separate channel. Plan beats strictly from the materials below.\n\nA beat is NOT a summary.\nA beat is a DRAMATIC PRESSURE UNIT that can immediately become a scene.\n\nEvery beat must create pressure.\n\n=========================================\nSTORY BIBLE:\n\n'+('Story: "'+a9+'" (tone: '+b1.d+", target age: "+b1.c+")")+"\n\n"+a+"\n\n=========================================\nCODEX (compressed / references):\n\n"+a0+"\n\n=========================================\nCHAPTER METADATA:\n\n"+e+" "+a3+"\n"+c+" "+a1+"\n"+b+" "+a2+u.N+A.blg(b7)+"\n\n"+a5+u.N+a7+'\n\n=========================================\nSCREENWRITER REQUIREMENTS (apply to EVERY beat):\n\n- OBJECTIVE: "goal" must state a clear, active, scene-doable want \u2014 what the character tries to accomplish on screen in the moment (verbs, tactics, concrete stakes).\n- FAILED ATTEMPT: across goal + conflict + turn, show trying that does NOT cleanly succeed \u2014 blockage, partial win at a cost, retry, or plan blown up. Smooth success without friction is wrong.\n- INTERRUPTIONS: prefer external cuts \u2014 someone enters, a message lands, an obligation interrupts, the environment fights back, a deadline hits, an argument erupts.\n- UNRESOLVED TURN: "turn" must end on a hook \u2014 new complication, stalled progress, worse stakes, redirected focus, or a forced choice before closure. Do not resolve the beat\'s tension in a tidy emotional or thematic paragraph.\n\nEach beat must:\n- generate playable scene behavior (a director can block it with actors and camera),\n- contain visible conflict (opposition the audience can see or hear),\n- contain a visible turn (a concrete change in situation, tactic, or momentum \u2014 on screen).\n\nEvery beat must create pressure.\n\n=========================================\nTASK:\n\nCreate 4\u20138 beats.\n\nEach beat must create pressure.\nEach beat must interrupt or complicate an objective, change the situation, and leave something unresolved for the next beat.\n\n=========================================\nMANDATORY BEAT STRUCTURE:\n\nEach beat MUST contain:\n\n- title\n- goal\n- conflict\n- turn\n\n=========================================\nBEAT WRITING RULES:\n\nGOAL (on-screen objective):\nState what the character is actively trying to DO right now \u2014 specific, playable, interruptible.\n\nGOOD:\n"\u0422\u0438\u043c\u0443\u0440 \u043f\u044b\u0442\u0430\u0435\u0442\u0441\u044f \u0437\u0430\u043f\u0438\u0441\u0430\u0442\u044c \u0432\u0441\u0442\u0443\u043f\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0434\u043b\u044f \u0432\u043b\u043e\u0433\u0430"\n\nBAD:\n"\u0422\u0438\u043c\u0443\u0440 \u0445\u043e\u0447\u0435\u0442 \u0432\u044b\u0440\u0430\u0437\u0438\u0442\u044c \u0441\u0435\u0431\u044f"\n\n-----------------------------------------\n\nCONFLICT (visible opposition + friction):\nMust actively interfere with the goal. Prefer a failed attempt, an interruption, or mounting resistance \u2014 not a lecture or a mood paragraph.\n\nDraw conflict from:\n- another person blocking or demanding,\n- time pressure,\n- environment,\n- competing priorities,\n- missing objects,\n- interruptions,\n- rules,\n- embarrassment,\n- physical obstacles.\n\nConflict must be visible on screen (action, dialogue, timing, props, space).\n\n-----------------------------------------\n\nTURN (unresolved shift, not closure):\nThe turn is a SHIFT, not a resolution and not a theme statement.\n\nThe beat must end with at least one of:\n- a complication,\n- redirected attention,\n- new pressure,\n- changed tactic,\n- worsened situation,\n- unexpected interruption,\n- a behavioral decision under duress.\n\nThe turn must be visible on screen.\n\nGOOD:\n"\u0422\u0438\u043c\u0443\u0440 \u0443\u0431\u0438\u0440\u0430\u0435\u0442 \u0442\u0435\u043b\u0435\u0444\u043e\u043d \u0438 \u043d\u0430\u0447\u0438\u043d\u0430\u0435\u0442 \u0437\u0430\u043f\u0438\u0441\u044b\u0432\u0430\u0442\u044c \u0438\u0434\u0435\u0438 \u0432 \u0431\u043b\u043e\u043a\u043d\u043e\u0442"\n\nBAD:\n"\u0422\u0438\u043c\u0443\u0440 \u043f\u043e\u043d\u0438\u043c\u0430\u0435\u0442 \u0446\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u044c \u0441\u0435\u043c\u044c\u0438"\n\n=========================================\nFORBIDDEN (screenwriter discipline \u2014 do not use in any field):\n\n- emotional interpretation (no explaining inner feelings, realizations about self, "the weight of\u2026", therapeutic language)\n- thematic summary (no "this represents", "the theme is", "symbolically", moral-of-the-story framing)\n- literary language (no lyrical metaphors, essay voice, purple prose, literary narration)\n\nAlso DO NOT:\n- explain emotions as abstract commentary,\n- describe themes or lessons,\n- summarize character arcs in evaluative voice,\n- state morals,\n- write prose narration or long interior monologue,\n- resolve tension within the same beat,\n- use realization verbs as the turn: "\u043f\u043e\u043d\u0438\u043c\u0430\u0435\u0442", "\u043e\u0441\u043e\u0437\u043d\u0430\u0451\u0442", "\u0447\u0443\u0432\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0443\u0435\u0442", "\u043f\u0440\u0438\u043d\u0438\u043c\u0430\u0435\u0442" (and equivalents in other languages).\n\nAvoid abstraction.\n\nPrefer:\nphysical behavior, dialogue beats, timing, space, objects > interpretation.\n\n=========================================\nSCENE ENERGY RULE:\n\nEvery beat must create pressure.\n\nAdditionally, each beat must contain at least ONE of:\n- interruption,\n- failed attempt,\n- argument,\n- misunderstanding,\n- pressure increase,\n- decision under friction,\n- competing objective.\n\nIf the beat feels calm, complete, literary, or emotionally summarized, it is wrong.\n\n=========================================\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON array.\n\n[\n{\n"title": "...",\n"goal": "...",\n"conflict": "...",\n"turn": "..."\n}\n]\n\n=========================================\nREFERENCE EXAMPLE:\n\n{\n"title": "\u0421\u0431\u0438\u0442\u043e\u0435 \u0432\u0441\u0442\u0443\u043f\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435",\n"goal": "\u0422\u0438\u043c\u0443\u0440 \u043f\u044b\u0442\u0430\u0435\u0442\u0441\u044f \u0437\u0430\u043f\u0438\u0441\u0430\u0442\u044c \u043a\u0440\u0430\u0441\u0438\u0432\u043e\u0435 \u0432\u0441\u0442\u0443\u043f\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0434\u043b\u044f \u0432\u043b\u043e\u0433\u0430",\n"conflict": "\u0434\u043e\u043c \u0448\u0443\u043c\u0438\u0442, \u0410\u0434\u0435\u043b\u0438\u043d\u0430 \u043b\u0435\u0437\u0435\u0442 \u0432 \u043a\u0430\u0434\u0440, \u0430 \u0410\u043b\u0438\u044f \u043f\u043e\u0441\u0442\u043e\u044f\u043d\u043d\u043e \u0434\u0430\u0451\u0442 \u0435\u043c\u0443 \u043f\u043e\u0440\u0443\u0447\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f",\n"turn": "\u0422\u0438\u043c\u0443\u0440 \u0431\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0430\u0435\u0442 \u043f\u043e\u043f\u044b\u0442\u043a\u0438 \u0441\u043d\u044f\u0442\u044c \u0432\u0441\u0442\u0443\u043f\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0434\u043e\u043c\u0430 \u0438 \u0440\u0435\u0448\u0430\u0435\u0442 \u0438\u0441\u043a\u0430\u0442\u044c \u043c\u0430\u0442\u0435\u0440\u0438\u0430\u043b \u0443\u0436\u0435 \u0432 \u0434\u043e\u0440\u043e\u0433\u0435"\n}\n\n=========================================\n\n'+d+"\n"
